WEBVTT

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.320
But we don't even have enough materials

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:11.720
While we had briefly been depressed, now we worked full of hope at the thought...

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.360
...that heading for the mainland might open a new road for us.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:23.660
We built a hut near the ruins for convenience while we worked, and began our life there.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:33.110
And we finished collecting parts to build our new ship with.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.960
It's no good, it's no good!

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.550
It won't do at all!

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.540
Can't you tell by looking?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.860
But you said it was fine before...

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.060
Don't give me buts!

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.450
Considering the list of parts you've been able to collect, you have some plan?

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.260
I thought to separately generate both the floatation and the propulsion with the gravity control unit.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.760
I think it's pretty good.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:01.950
That's the weakest part!

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:08.480
Where's the expected laden weight of the ship, and the power output for the gravity control unit?

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:09.900
I was going to do those next...

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:10.990
What?

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:17.480
I mean, we don't have the materials gathered, so we can't calculate our laden weight.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.560
And we don't know the power of the gravity control unit.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.540
However you estimate it, no matter the weight, the power is insufficent.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.440
A blueprint for a ship that won't work is just a scrap of paper!

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.940
But we don't even have enough materials. What should we do?

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.940
It's your job to think about that.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.200
Make the ship drastically lighter...

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.580
...or devise a way to power up the gravity control unit...

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:51.560
...or find another gravity control unit somehow.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.040
If you can't solve the components problem, it's pointless to draw a blueprint.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:02.700
Looks like it didn't go well.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.910
These kinds of things happen.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:09.450
Shingo...

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.640
It's okay. Cheer up!

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.260
I'm not upset or anything.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.510
That's good to hear.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.930
Anyways, perhaps it's sudden, but what work should we do today?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.510
I want everybody to split up again and look for gravity-related parts.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:27.900
Gravity-related?

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.860
Right now we won't have enough power.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:37.130
So I want parts we can use to power it up, or if possible, another gravity control unit.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:39.230
We've already searched so much we're sick of it!

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.850
There must be places we still haven't searched yet!

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.650
I'm just not suited to garbage collection.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.250
Oh, but I think it's fun, like searching for treasure!

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.510
It must be an easy life, being a fairy-tale girl.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.510
Fairy-tale girl?

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:57.900
Hey!

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:01.010
We were just havin' the morning meetin'.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.360
We're back.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:03.530
We had a big catch.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.660
It's true, wow!

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.400
Let's hurry up and make breakfast!

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.180
Yeah, yeah. Help us out, Ms. Fairy-tale.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.800
Oh, Chako! Yeesh...

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.330
So me, Sharla, Adam, and Chako will look for food...

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.210
...and the rest of you, please take care of the parts search.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.430
I'll search to the west.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.080
Then I'll take the south.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:28.500
I'll do the north.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.980
Howard and I will search to the east.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.250
Aye-aye.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.760
Be careful, everyone.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.430
We'll meet up in the afternoon.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.360
No use...

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:18.750
There really is nothing here. Let's give up and go home.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:20.890
Let's search a little longer.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.540
I'm already tired.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.390
You got tired right away.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.130
This is useless too.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.050
This is great!

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.640
Maybe I can use this to make a hammock to sleep in!

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:38.510
For crying out loud!

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.380
Howard! What are you goofing off at?

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.130
Can't you tell by looking? I wasn't goofing off!

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.450
If you're going to complain, tell it to the wind!

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.800
Complain to the wind?

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.060
That's right! There's that possibility!

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.840
Howard, there were other sheets, right?

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.210
Yeah. You said we might be able to use them for something, so we took them all.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:05.030
That's it! Howard, that's it!

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.580
Are you okay?

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:12.340
So there weren't any parts that seemed usable?

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.040
All there was were fragments of panels.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.890
There was nothing to do with power generation.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:21.300
We had a great haul on the food side.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.160
See, look!

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.340
It's just Shingo and Howard left.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.290
They're back!

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:27.560
Hey!

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.410
Look, seems like they had a good result.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:33.150
How was it?

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:34.950
No use, nothing at all.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:38.330
But I thought up a good idea!

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.910
I'll draw it up and show it to you!

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.010
Everyone can hold off on searching for parts!

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.700
Things are about to get busy!

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:47.780
Is he <i>really</i> okay?

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:56.740
Wait...

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.260
I have to make it able to work when the direction is bad.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:01.540
Hey!

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.290
So you were in a place like this. Come get food.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.950
I'll have it later! Sorry!

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:10.730
Everyone's waiting!

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:12.600
Don't bug me right now!

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:16.390
Then we'll just eat without you. Do whatever you want.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.890
Now everyone, we'll have Ms. Fairy-Tale announce the name of this hut!

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.840
Let's see... the Angels' Hut!

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:27.860
I've built model yachts before.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:32.030
And I thought, "Why not use the structure of a yacht for this ship?"

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:40.510
If we do this, we can use most of the gravity control unit's power for floatation...

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.780
...so we should be able to overcome the shortage of power.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.640
We can use the wind's power for propulsion--

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:46.650
Idiot!

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.000
This is different from building some toy!

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:57.450
What point is there in making a vehicle lacking in safety and certainty?

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:07.350
Making something you can rely on even if you lose power or run out of energy is a mechanic's job.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.950
I can't vouch for its certainty, but safety is no problem.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:16.050
If you make it like this, it'll fall right over when a strong wind blows.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:22.050
If we use a gyrostabilizer in place of a keel, we'll be fine.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.530
What will you do when there's no wind?

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:28.690
We'll only have a little self-propulsion if there's no wind at all...

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.550
...but look here. If we make the mast able to move to suit the wind's direction...

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.820
...as long as there's even a little wind, we can use it for propulsion.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.260
So in the end, it drifts in the wind?

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:43.800
But because we'll be floating due to the gravity control unit...

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:47.470
...we should be able to achieve a fair speed using just the wind.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:53.400
There's no pride for a mechanic in making a ship that drifts in the wind.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.410
But...

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:58.460
But it'll be impossible to find another gravity control unit...

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:01.430
...and I've already done the calculations to power up as much as we can.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.450
This is the best we can do with the parts we have!

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.450
Young'uns always give up so quickly.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:08.100
You don't have enough willpower.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:09.900
Give the willpower a rest!

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.200
Mr. Porte, you said it before yourself, didn't you?

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:21.080
What's important for a mechanic is to consider the situation on the spot and think of the best action.

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:23.510
You can't think this is the best course.

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:25.580
Adults are always like that!

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:30.560
Sticking to their own experiences and never trying out new concepts!

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:34.050
Shut up! I don't want to hear that from an amateur like you!

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:39.150
Take this and get out of here.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:06.750
You forgot this.

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:08.360
Take it.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:10.160
Throw it away.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.550
Is that okay? You put your best into it.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:19.880
I've made all the adjustments I can.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:24.560
I've increased the efficiency of the gravity control unit.

00:11:25.310 --> 00:11:28.310
It should increase the power by 10%.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:34.280
And if we use a structure like this, the total weight will drop by 5%.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:37.650
So there was a trick like this!

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:41.250
But is it okay to start building it based on this?

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.400
Right now, you're this thing's main mechanic.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.580
Do whatever you want with it.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:48.490
I'll give it a try!

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.550
If I come to another dead end, will you give me advice like you have until now?

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.190
Well, only if you ask me real nice.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:00.680
I wish I hadn't asked.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:03.940
You have a good spirit, kid.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:25.090
Ready, set...

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:42.230
This ain't good... no response at all.

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:44.700
Shingo, what would you do?

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:50.400
Right, if only we hadn't intentionally structured it so bothersomely.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:51.900
Wait a second.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:56.890
Maybe we should have had this shortcut here and connect directly.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.010
Exactly. Simple is best.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:01.580
Right!

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:02.930
Here, try it out.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:03.600
Right.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.490
Pretty impressive!

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:13.050
Lookin' at you two, it's almost like scholar 'n pupil!

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:15.110
Did you just realize that now?

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.190
Yeesh, it's no fair being stuck with a pupil who does such shoddy work!

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:20.810
That's not true!

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:39.000
For the following week, we split up and worked all day at making the ship.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.260
And after that, there.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.740
Look at my magnificent movement.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:12.730
Considering the predicted speed of the ship and the distance to the mainland...

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:14.740
...we calculated the length of the voyage to be at most ten days.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:19.730
We decided to take two weeks' worth of preserved food, to make sure we would have extra.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:27.040
And after about another week passed, we were ready to have a trial run of the gravity system.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:53.250
We did it!

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:00.190
We've done it!

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.950
We've finally made it this far.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.960
After so long, my mechanic's blood is on fire.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.180
It's not like we're done yet, though.

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:16.950
That's certainly true.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:20.630
Hey, keep teaching me things from now on, okay?

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:22.430
I don't mind...

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:27.080
...but I've been taught things by you as well.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:28.540
By me?

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:35.360
When you first showed me that blueprint with the yacht drawn on it.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:42.980
I got mad without thinking, but I was troubled because I didn't come up with an idea like that.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.450
Just like you said.

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:52.200
I considered my experience to be important, and hesitated to adopt a flexible way of thinking.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:57.260
When I thought you'd figured that out, I yelled without thinking.

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:00.980
I might have spoken impertinently.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:04.650
No, that much is just right for a youngster.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:07.690
Right?

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:15.190
If I had been able to think that way, I wouldn't have lost my son.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.740
Mr. Porte, you had a son?

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:37.690
Once, I ran a small repair shop with my son in a corner of colony Muwa-C.

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.310
Pops, look at that!

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:47.850
It's the latest model of settlement ship!

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:53.700
Amazing.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:58.400
One day, my son talked to me about wanting to board the newest model of settlement ship...

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:02.140
...which was in town for repairs, as a mechanic.

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:07.500
As a stubborn and obstinate craftsman, I rebuked him immediately.

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:14.700
No way! Anyway, what do you think you'll be able to do if you get on a computer-run ship like that?

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:18.680
Where do you see ships <i>not</i> run by computers nowadays?

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:20.400
They're just plain better.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.250
You should let the computer take care of the things it can!

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:25.130
Be a little more flexible!

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:29.810
Getting used by the computer to do what it says... what makes that a mechanic?

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:32.290
I don't want to hear an amateur like you talking like you know everything!

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:36.710
You might call me an amateur, but there are people who value my work!

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:42.110
And I won't spend my life stuck in a small town like you have!

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:43.880
Shut up!

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:45.190
What about Mom?

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:48.410
Covered in grease from morning until night, just working!

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:51.490
She died without having a moment of fun in her life!

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:53.490
I don't want to be that way!

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:56.140
Get out of here!

00:17:57.410 --> 00:17:59.250
If you want to board a ship, then go ahead!

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:01.910
But never come back here again!

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:07.200
You're old, so don't push yourself too hard.

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:15.110
That's all?

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:15.710
Yes.

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:16.990
Why?

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:20.810
You didn't mean it when you told him never to come back.

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:22.840
There was an accident.

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:23.780
An accident?

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:27.080
Continuing, news about the accident.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:33.980
Late last night, the newest settlement ship lost control due to a main computer failure.

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:37.310
It sustained serious damage after colliding with a satellite.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:41.360
There is little hope for the survival of the passengers and crew.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:44.180
We will now list their names.

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:48.750
Please have sympathy for their friends and families.

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:53.180
My son's name was there.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:56.400
I couldn't believe my eyes.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:59.480
But it was the grave truth.

00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:03.640
But maybe he was still alive.

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:10.760
In baseless hope that perhaps he had reached another colony somewhere in an escape shuttle...

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.490
...I asked around for news of my son.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.850
But in the end nobody knew anything about him.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:22.090
It seemed I had truly been left alone.

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:23.930
Truly.

00:19:33.190 --> 00:19:37.730
I sold the shop and boarded a transport ship as a mechanic.

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:43.430
I felt that as long as I was travelling through space, I might encounter my son someday.

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:48.710
And at each destination, I asked about my son.

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:54.190
There wasn't anything else I could do.

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:57.940
No, I don't know.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:13.460
From Titan, a -180ЃЃЃЃ° frozen world, I saw Saturn's rings.

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:22.460
At Andromeda upsilon, we stayed a week near a gas storm that had a strange light from its center.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:30.340
At 51 Pegasi, I saw a hot Jupiter with eerie patterns made by strong winds.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:37.950
What made all these experiences possible was visiting all those places to search for my son.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:45.680
It was like after having been tied to a single thing, my eyes had been opened to a new world.

00:20:57.190 --> 00:20:59.250
So something like that happened.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:05.810
After this, I'll probably keep travelling in search of my son.

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:11.180
Whoever they are, it seems everyone has something they have to chase after.

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:12.860
Mr. Porte...

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:18.790
If I stop, I'll lose the hope even to keep living.

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:22.910
I guess that's what they call life.

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:28.130
That's kind of amazing, Mr. Porte.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:31.390
What do you mean? You're the amazing ones.

00:21:32.030 --> 00:21:36.040
You've survived this long with nothing on this planet.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:38.930
Because we have each other.

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:47.630
Maybe my son is also surviving with his shipmates on some undiscovered planet.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:49.650
That's what I think.

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:52.240
That's right! He's definitely still alive somewhere!

00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:57.110
It seems like I was the one who was cheered up!

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:58.280
Ah well.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:04.350
If you want them, I'll pass all my skills on to you.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:07.730
Alright!

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:10.880
Alright, alright! Alright!

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:15.710
Well, it's pretty late. Let's get to sleep.

00:22:15.710 --> 00:22:16.200
Right!

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:19.650
Huh? Mr. Porte?

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:20.810
Are you okay?

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:26.510
Yeah, I'm fine. It just seems my wounds aren't fully healed yet.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:28.580
But no need to worry!

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:30.000
That's all it is!

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.960
Now hurry up and get to sleep!

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.510
The elderly wake up early in the morning!

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:36.900
Yeah, okay.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:37.800
Good night.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:40.730
Yeah, good night.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.510
Finally... flared up...

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:07.540
Mr. Porte is strict, but he's really a kind person.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:12.230
Yeah, yeah. He's got a bad mouth, but he takes care of what need to be taken care of.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:14.660
We've all been counting on Mr. Porte.

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:18.260
Getting the ship finished was thanks to Mr. Porte too!

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:19.690
Yep.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.590
Stay with us forever, ol' man Porte.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:29.660
Next time: A very important friend.
